      "answer": "Based on the provided context, here is a precise architectural map of the repository system, structured to reflect the evidence from the README.md documentation and Docker configuration files:\n\n---\n\n### **Core Architecture Overview**\nThe system implements a **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)** pipeline with 5 application services and 1 database, communicating via HTTP over Docker's internal network. Key components and their roles:\n\n| **Component**               | **Local Port** | **Primary Function**                                                                 |\n|-----------------------------|----------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Gradio (UI)                 | 7860            | User-facing interface for ingestion/query operations                              |\n| Ingestion Service           | 8001            | Data parsing, artifact graph construction, and structural inventory                |\n| Vector Store Service        | 8002            | Embedding storage and passage search (RAG vector database)                        |\n| LLM Service                 | 8003            | Text generation and model provider connections (e.g., OpenAI, Hugging Face)       |\n| RAG Orchestrator            | 8004            | Coordinates retrieval, evidence selection, and final response generation           |\n| PostgreSQL                  | N/A             | Stores graph metadata and document inventory (accessed by Ingestion/Vector Services) |\n\n---\n\n### **Critical Communication Flow**\n1. **User Interaction**  \n   \u2192 Gradio (Port 7860) initiates:  \n   - Ingestion operations \u2192 Ingestion Service (Port 8001)  \n   - Query operations \u2192 RAG Orchestrator (Port 8004)\n\n2. **RAG Orchestrator Workflow** (Port 8004)  \n   - Queries **Ingestion Service** (Port 8001) for graph metadata  \n   - Queries **Vector Store Service** (Port 8002) for relevant passages  \n   - Queries **LLM Service** (Port 8003) for final response generation\n\n3. **Ingestion Service Workflow** (Port 8001)  \n   - Sends embeddings \u2192 **Vector Store Service** (Port 8002)  \n   - Sends graph metadata \u2192 **PostgreSQL** (for persistent storage)\n\n4. **Vector Store Service** (Port 8002)  \n   - Interacts with **PostgreSQL** for vector storage/search operations\n\n5. **LLM Service** (Port 8003)  \n   - Connects to external model providers (e.g., OpenAI API, Hugging Face)\n\n---\n\n### **Docker Network Configuration** (from `shared/config/service_urls.py`)\nAll services communicate via *internal Docker network URLs* (port 8000 is the **communication port** for service-to-service calls):\n\n| **Service**                  | **Internal Docker URL**                     | **Purpose**                                  |\n|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| Ingestion Service            | `http://ingestion_service:8000`            | Data ingestion pipeline                     |\n| Vector Store Service         | `http://vector_store_service:8002`          | Vector storage/search                      |\n| LLM Service                  | `http://llm_service:8000`                  | Text generation                            |\n| RAG Orchestrator             | `http://rag_orchestrator:8000`             | Pipeline coordination                      |\n\n> **Note**: The `8000` port in Docker configuration is *not* the local port (e.g., 8001/8002). It is the **internal communication port** used by services to interact with each other *within the Docker network*.\n\n---\n\n### **Why This Architecture?**\n- **RAG Compliance**: Explicitly follows the RAG pattern (retrieval \u2192 generation) with vector search for contextual relevance.\n- **Docker-Optimized**: Services are isolated via Docker networks with standardized internal URLs (no external IPs).\n- **Real-World Alignment**: Matches FastAPI stack (common for RAG systems) and PostgreSQL for metadata storage.\n- **No External Dependencies**: All communication happens within Docker (no public network exposure).\n\nThis map is **directly derived from the provided context** (README.md table, Docker config files) and avoids external assumptions. The key insight: **Local ports (8001\u20138004) are for user-facing exposure, while internal Docker URLs (8000) handle service-to-service communication**.\n\nFor implementation details, refer to:  \n- `README.md` (tables/diagrams)  \n- `shared/config/service_urls.py` (Docker network configuration)",
